---
const title = 'OptiPrune — Prove what your code uses';
---
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="description" content="OptiPrune proves what your TypeScript and JavaScript workspace actually uses." />
    <link rel="icon" href="/assets/optiprune-logo.svg" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles.css" />
    <title>{title}</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="ambient ambient-a"></div><div class="ambient ambient-b"></div><div class="grid-noise"></div>
    <header class="site-header">
      <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="OptiPrune home"><img src="/assets/optiprune-logo.svg" alt="" /><span>OPTI<span>PRUNE</span></span></a>
      <nav class="nav" aria-label="Primary"><a href="/docs">Docs</a><a href="/plugins">Plugins</a><a href="/blog">Blog</a><a href="https://github.com/optiprune/core">GitHub</a></nav>
      <button class="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Toggle color mode" data-theme-toggle><span>◐</span><span class="toggle-label">Color mode</span></button>
    </header>
    <div id="announcement" class="announcement" hidden></div>
    <main class="page">
      <section class="hero"><div><div class="kicker">Static analysis · TypeScript + JavaScript</div><h1>Stop guessing.<br /><em>Start proving.</em></h1><p class="lede">OptiPrune builds a dependency graph from your real entry points and shows what is never reached — files, imports, exports, packages, and logic.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="/docs">Read the docs →</a><a class="button secondary" href="/plugins">Explore plugins</a></div><p class="blog-meta">npm install --save-dev @optiprune/cli · zero runtime dependencies · CI-ready</p></div><div class="hero-art"><img src="/assets/optiprune-animation.svg" alt="OptiPrune dependency graph animation" /></div></section>
      <div class="metrics"><div class="metric"><strong>7</strong><span>analysis layers</span></div><div class="metric"><strong>3</strong><span>confidence levels</span></div><div class="metric"><strong>0</strong><span>runtime dependencies</span></div><div class="metric"><strong id="plugin-count">—</strong><span>ecosystem plugins</span></div></div>
      <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="kicker">Why OptiPrune</div><h2>Evidence over intuition.</h2></div><p>Designed for safe cleanup: inspect the graph, review confidence, then remove only what your codebase can prove is unreachable.</p></div><div class="feature-grid"><div class="card"><span class="number">01 / GRAPH</span><h3>Reachability, not heuristics</h3><p>Entry points create a real module graph instead of relying on naming guesses or arbitrary percentages.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="number">02 / CONTEXT</span><h3>Context-aware findings</h3><p>Dynamic dispatch, test conventions, generated files, and package metadata stay visible in the analysis.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="number">03 / CONTROL</span><h3>Dry-run first</h3><p>Every fix is explicit. Preview changes, use confidence gates, and keep a clean audit trail in CI.</p></div></div></section>
      <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="kicker">A small proof</div><h2>Scan. Verify. Prune.</h2></div></div><div class="terminal"><div>$ npx optiprune ./src</div><div class="warn">✖ [HIGH] src/legacy/orphan.ts is not reachable from any entry point</div><div class="warn">⚠ [MEDIUM] export 'legacyFn' is never imported</div><div class="ok">✓ 2 safe to remove · 1 needs review</div></div></section>
    </main>
    <footer class="site-footer"><span>MIT License · DreamLongYT</span><span>Built for teams that ship less dead code.</span><a href="/docs">Read the documentation →</a></footer>
    <script src="/site.js" defer></script>
  </body>
</html>
